3:59Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "6.\tThe Farsi word X may be the single-most prolific foreign word in the English language. English borrowed it directly as a royal title. X, traveling indirectly through Arabic, Spanish, and Old French, gave English a remarkable cluster of other words. The game called Y in English got its modern name from this Persian root, because the central piece of the game is X. From Y, English derived the word Z, originally a term meaning to threaten the opposing king. Z got generalized to mean “to stop, restrain, or verify,” and from there to “a mark of verification.” The pattern of alternating squares on a Y-board gave us “Z-ed” fabric. Even the slip of paper you write at the bank — Z, in American spelling — comes from this same root, originally a counterfoil that “Z-ed” the validity of a payment. Medieval English royal accounts were tallied by moving counters around on a Y-board-patterned cloth. This gave the royal treasury its name A, preserved today in the title \"Chancellor of the A,\" the British government's finance minister. ID X (5 points), Y (5 points), Z (5 points), and A (5 points).", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:44:07Z", "username": "Nikhil Borra", "text": "Validate", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:44:13Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Shah", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:44:15Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Chess", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:44:25Z", "username": "Shyam Gopal", "text": "Check", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-29T14:44:26Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Exchequer", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, 